const e={intro:["Christianity is a religion of the word, and for its first two centuries it left almost no pictures. What it left instead were marks: a fish scratched on a tomb slab, an anchor cut beside a name, a letter of the Greek alphabet doubled over on itself in a scribe's hand. The symbols gathered here are therefore not illustrations of a doctrine already settled. They are, in many cases, the earliest surviving evidence of what the doctrine was, and they are older than most of the words used to defend it.","Two honesties govern this hall. The first is chronological: the cross arrives late as an image, and the crucifixion is almost unrepresented in Christian art before the fifth century. The oldest surviving depiction of a man on a cross with a Christian referent is generally reckoned to be a piece of mockery scratched into plaster on the Palatine — though a rival claim is made for a scribe's ligature, and it is set out below. The second honesty is genealogical: a great many of these signs were not invented but inherited. The shepherd with a ram on his shoulders was carved in Greece before Christ was born; the peacock carried Roman empresses to heaven; the ankh was Egyptian for three thousand years before a Copt cut one on a Christian stele. Borrowing is not a scandal here. It is the method.","Where the traditions differ, they are given as differing, in their own terms. The Assyrian Church of the East is not called Nestorian; the Oriental Orthodox are miaphysite, not monophysite; the Old Believers are not raskolniki. Where a tradition's account of an object's origin is not the historians' account, both are set down, attributed, and left standing beside each other. Where a legend is a legend — the pelican that opens her breast, the vine cuttings bound with the Virgin's hair — it is named as one, without being sneered at. Nothing here is quoted that was not written, and nothing dated that is not datable."],entries:[{slug:"cross",name:"The Cross",glyph:"✝︎",form:"Two beams crossed: the Latin cross (crux immissa) with the transom set high, the Greek cross with four equal arms, the tau (crux commissa) with no upright above the bar, the saltire laid diagonally. Heraldry and liturgy between them count several hundred further variants.",origin:"The instrument was Roman, common, and shameful; the image came late. No object that can be securely identified as a Christian cross survives from before the third century, and the crucifixion itself is almost unrepresented in Christian art until the fifth. Before then the cross is made rather than drawn: Tertullian (De corona 3, usually dated c. 211) describes Christians tracing it on the forehead at every turn of the day, and Justin Martyr (1 Apology 55) finds its shape hidden in the ship's mast, the plough, the carpenter's tools and the standards of Rome. The earliest datable narrative crucifixions are two small Roman works of the 420s and 430s: a carved panel of the cypress doors of Santa Sabina, and an ivory casket panel — one of the Maskell Ivories — now in the British Museum.",meaning:"The manner of the death, and the claim that the manner of the death was the victory. In the East it is more often shown gemmed, haloed or flowering — a trophy rather than a gallows — and it is signed on the body, breathed over water, and set at the crossroads.",usedBy:"Every Christian tradition without exception, though the form, the number of bars, the direction of the hand that signs it and the words said over it differ from rite to rite.",note:"That the crucifixion is scarcely depicted for four centuries is not proof of embarrassment, but the ancient hostile witnesses assumed embarrassment: the pagan speaker in Minucius Felix's Octavius throws cross-worship at the Christians as an accusation, and is answered that they neither worship crosses nor wish for them. Unicode encodes only a handful of the forms, and its names are worth quoting exactly: U+271D LATIN CROSS ✝, U+271A HEAVY GREEK CROSS ✚, U+2720 MALTESE CROSS ✠, U+2628 CROSS OF LORRAINE ☨, U+2613 SALTIRE ☓, U+2626 ORTHODOX CROSS ☦, U+2629 CROSS OF JERUSALEM ☩. A thin selection from a vocabulary that runs to hundreds — and note that the two-barred cross of Lorraine and the patriarchal cross are related but not identical forms, of which Unicode names only the first."},{slug:"alexamenos-graffito",name:"The Alexamenos Graffito",glyph:null,form:"A crude scratch in plaster: a man with the head of a donkey hangs on a cross, a second small figure stands to the left with one arm raised, and beneath runs the Greek line ΑΛΕΞΑΜΕΝΟϹ ϹΕΒΕΤΕ ΘΕΟΝ — Alexamenos worships God — the verb misspelt (σέβετε for σέβεται), as a bored hand would misspell it.",origin:"Cut into the wall of the Paedagogium on the Palatine Hill in Rome, a training house for imperial pages, and usually dated around the year 200; it is now in the Palatine Antiquarium. It stands near the head of the visual record of the crucifixion — earlier than any surviving Christian image of the same scene.",meaning:"Mockery. The donkey-headed god was a standing slander: Tertullian answers it directly in the Apology and in Ad nationes, and Minucius Felix has his pagan speaker repeat it. The graffito is a schoolboy jeering at a fellow page.",usedBy:"No one, devotionally. It is used by historians as one of the earliest surviving depictions of the crucifixion made by anyone, and by Christian writers as evidence of what the claim cost socially in the second century.",note:"A second graffito reported from a nearby room reads Alexamenos fidelis — Alexamenos is faithful — and is often presented as his reply; the two were not found on the same wall, and the connection between them is an inference, not a documented fact. The dating range offered by scholars runs across the late second and third centuries; c. 200 is the usual compromise. Its primacy is not absolute: the staurogram claim below, and a handful of engraved gems whose dates are contested, are the rival candidates."},{slug:"crucifix",name:"The Crucifix",glyph:null,form:"The cross bearing the body. Two great types divide the history: the Christus triumphans, alive, upright, eyes open, robed in a long sleeved colobium or crowned as king; and the Christus patiens, dead, head fallen, knees bent, the body's weight visible.",origin:"The living, robed Christ comes first. The full-page crucifixion of the Rabbula Gospels, a Syriac manuscript whose colophon dates it to 586 at the monastery of Beth Zagba, shows him clothed and open-eyed. The dead Christ, slumped and suffering, is a Byzantine development of roughly the tenth and eleventh centuries, and the great early Western monument of the type is the oak Gero Crucifix in Cologne Cathedral, c. 965-970.",meaning:"In the triumphant type, the reign begun from the tree. In the suffering type, the cost — and, in Western devotion from the twelfth century onward, an object for compassion rather than acclamation.",usedBy:"Latin Catholic, Anglican and Lutheran churches keep the corpus freely. Eastern Orthodox and Oriental Orthodox usage prefers the painted or low-relief cross to the sculpture in the round; the Assyrian Church of the East and most Reformed churches use a cross without a body, for reasons each states differently.",note:"Preference is doctrinal, not decorative. Many Reformed communities read a bare cross as testimony to the resurrection and a corpus as a lingering at the tomb; many Catholics read a bare cross as an evasion of the incarnation. Both accounts are offered in their own words here, and neither is the historical origin of the practice, which is chiefly regional."},{slug:"staurogram",name:"The Staurogram (Tau-Rho)",glyph:"⳨",form:"A tau crossed by a rho, the loop of the rho sitting at the top of the upright so that the compendium reads, to a sympathetic eye, as a head above a crossbeam.",origin:"A Greek scribal ligature already in use for other purposes, taken into Christian manuscripts inside the abbreviations of σταυρός (cross) and σταυρόω (to crucify). It appears in that use in three of the oldest substantial New Testament papyri: P66 and P75, conventionally dated around the year 200 and to the late second or early third century, and P45 of the third.",meaning:"The word cross, written; and — on one reading — the thing itself, drawn.",usedBy:"Early Greek scribes; later Coptic manuscript and inscriptional practice, where Unicode encodes it in the Coptic block as U+2CE8 COPTIC SYMBOL TAU RO; and Latin scribes as an abbreviation.",note:"Kurt Aland first proposed that the tau-rho in these papyri is pictorial, and Larry Hurtado pressed the case at length (The Earliest Christian Artifacts, 2006): that it is a pictographic reference to the crucified Jesus, and therefore the earliest surviving visual reference to the crucifixion — earlier than the Alexamenos graffito, and made in reverence rather than mockery. The argument is well received but not settled: the ligature has pre-Christian and non-Christian uses, and a scribe may have written a compendium without seeing a picture in it. The conventional early dates of P66 and P75 have themselves been challenged."},{slug:"chi-rho",name:"The Chi-Rho",glyph:"☧",form:"Chi (Χ) and rho (Ρ) superimposed, the rho standing upright through the centre of the X, its loop at the top. Often set within a wreath, or with alpha and omega hanging from the arms.",origin:"The first two letters of ΧΡΙΣΤΟΣ. The same ligature already served Greek scribes as an abbreviation mark, notably for χρηστόν, marking a useful passage in the margin — so the monogram was in the world before it was Christian. Its imperial career begins under Constantine: a silver medallion struck at Ticinum about 315 shows the emperor's helmet bearing it, and it spreads across coinage, standards, sarcophagi and apse mosaics through the fourth century.",meaning:"The name and title in two letters. Where it stands inside a wreath the reference is to a Roman victory crown, and the sign is read as a trophy.",usedBy:"Ubiquitous in the fourth- and fifth-century Mediterranean, Latin and Greek alike; still used in Western vestments, altar furnishings and printing, and in Insular manuscript art.",note:"The Chi Rho Iota page of the Book of Kells (folio 34r) is the extreme case of the monogram absorbing a whole page: three letters, opening Matthew's account of the nativity at 1:18, elaborated until the text is nearly unreadable and the reading is meant to be slow. Whether Constantine's adoption was conviction, policy, or a deliberate ambiguity legible to Christians and to worshippers of the Unconquered Sun alike is argued still; his triumphal arch at Rome, dedicated in 315, carries no Christian sign at all. Unicode places the sign at U+2627 CHI RHO, among the miscellaneous symbols, and encodes the Coptic scribal form separately at U+2CE9 COPTIC SYMBOL KHI RO ⳩."},{slug:"labarum",name:"The Labarum",glyph:null,form:"A military standard: a long spear crossed by a transverse bar, from which hangs a square banner of purple cloth worked with gold, and above the whole a golden wreath enclosing the Chi-Rho.",origin:"Described in detail by Eusebius in the Life of Constantine (1.30-31), who says the emperor showed him the object itself. It is bound to the account of the vision before the battle at the Milvian Bridge on 28 October 312 — and the two earliest accounts of that vision do not agree.",meaning:"Victory given by, and owed to, the God of the Christians; the army's ensign converted, and with it the argument that the empire's success now rested on a new patron.",usedBy:"The Constantinian and later Roman armies; thereafter a standing image in Byzantine and Western art of imperial Christianity, and a heraldic device.",note:"Lactantius, writing within a few years of the battle (On the Deaths of the Persecutors 44), reports a dream on the eve of the fighting in which Constantine was told to mark the heavenly sign of God on his soldiers' shields, and describes a letter X with its top bent over — no daylight vision, no words in the sky, no banner. Eusebius, writing after Constantine's death some twenty-five years later, reports a cross of light seen above the sun about midday, as the day began to decline, by the whole army, with the Greek words τούτῳ νίκα, by this conquer, followed by a dream that night; he says he had it from the emperor under oath. Eusebius's own earlier Ecclesiastical History tells the battle without the vision. The familiar Latin in hoc signo vinces is a later rendering, not the phrase either source gives."},{slug:"crux-ansata",name:"The Crux Ansata",glyph:"☥︎",form:"A cross with a loop for its upper arm: the Egyptian ankh, redrawn by Christian hands with the loop sometimes squared, sometimes filled with a smaller cross.",origin:"Egyptian, the hieroglyph ꜥnḫ, life, in use for three millennia before it was Christian. Coptic Christians took it over directly — on funerary stelae, woven into textiles, cut into the walls of the temple at Philae when the sanctuary became a church.",meaning:"Life, and specifically eternal life; the inherited word made a promise of the resurrection.",usedBy:"The Coptic Orthodox Church of Alexandria above all, in manuscript, textile and stone; also in Nubian and Ethiopian material.",note:"Socrates Scholasticus (Ecclesiastical History 5.17) and Sozomen (7.15) both report that when the Serapeum at Alexandria was destroyed in 391 hieroglyphs shaped like crosses were found, and that Christians present read them as signifying the life to come — the earliest surviving account of the borrowing, told from the Christian side as a discovery rather than an appropriation. The Unicode position is worth stating precisely: the looped sign itself is encoded at U+2625 ANKH, in the Egyptological sense and outside the Coptic block, while the Coptic block carries the Christian scribal crosses — U+2CE7 COPTIC SYMBOL STAUROS ⳧, U+2CE8 COPTIC SYMBOL TAU RO ⳨, U+2CE9 COPTIC SYMBOL KHI RO ⳩."},{slug:"ichthys",name:"Ichthys, the Acrostic",glyph:"ΙΧΘΥΣ",form:"Five Greek capitals, sometimes written in a line, sometimes arranged as a wheel with the letters superimposed on a single hub.",origin:"ἰχθύς is simply the Greek for fish. Read as an acrostic it yields Ἰησοῦς Χριστὸς Θεοῦ Υἱὸς Σωτήρ — Jesus Christ, Son of God, Saviour. Augustine sets out the reading in the City of God (18.23), where he is discussing an acrostic in the Sibylline Oracles that spells the same words and adds ΣΤΑΥΡΟΣ. The epitaph of Pectorius at Autun, in Greek, carries the acrostic in the opening letters of its first five lines; it is variously dated across the third and fourth centuries.",meaning:"A creed compressed to five letters: the name, the title, the divine sonship, the office. It is a confession that can be written faster than it can be understood by anyone not already taught it.",usedBy:"Early Greek-speaking Christians; revived in the modern West, where it is more often the fish outline than the letters.",note:"The acrostic is a Greek accident and does not survive translation, which is why the fish becomes an unglossed picture in Latin, Coptic and Syriac use. Whether the acrostic generated the fish symbol or was devised afterwards to explain it is genuinely uncertain; both the eucharistic fish meals of the gospels and the ordinary Mediterranean fish sit behind the sign."},{slug:"fish",name:"The Fish",glyph:null,form:"Two curved strokes crossing at the tail to make a body and a fin — the whole animal in one movement of the hand. Often paired, or shown carrying a basket of bread on its back.",origin:"Among the commonest signs in the Roman catacombs and on early epitaphs, in use well before the cross. Clement of Alexandria, listing the seal-rings a Christian may lawfully wear (Paedagogus 3.11), names a dove, a fish, a ship running before the wind, a lyre, an anchor and a fisherman — our earliest explicit licence for the repertoire.",meaning:"Christ, by the acrostic; the baptised, by the water; the eucharist, by the loaves and fishes; and the apostles, by the calling of fishermen. The layers are not alternatives and the ancient sources use them together.",usedBy:"Early Christians throughout the Mediterranean; today chiefly as an identity badge in the modern West, where it has been enthusiastically parodied and counter-parodied.",note:"Tertullian, opening his treatise on baptism, calls Christians little fishes born in water after the manner of their ΙΧΘΥΣ — the earliest surviving Latin play on the acrostic. The popular story that the fish was a covert recognition sign, one stranger drawing an arc in the dust for another to complete, is not attested in any ancient source; it is a modern reconstruction, plausible in itself and unevidenced."},{slug:"alpha-omega",name:"Alpha and Omega",glyph:"Α Ω",form:"The first and last letters of the Greek alphabet, usually hung from the arms of a Chi-Rho or set flanking a cross or an enthroned figure; the omega is often written in its lunate rather than its capital form.",origin:"Revelation, where the phrase is spoken by the Lord God at 1:8, by the one seated on the throne at 21:6, and by Christ at 22:13. The pairing enters art in the fourth century and becomes near-universal on Latin and Greek sarcophagi, apses and coinage.",meaning:"Beginning and end, first and last, the whole of everything held between two letters; and — read alongside 22:13 — an ascription to Christ of what the same book has already said of God.",usedBy:"Everywhere, in every rite, and among the very few Greek elements retained untranslated in Latin, Coptic, Armenian and Slavonic art.",note:"The occurrence of the phrase at Revelation 1:11 is familiar from the Authorised Version but absent from the earliest manuscripts; it entered by way of the printed Greek text of the sixteenth century and is not printed in critical editions. The sign is also untranslatable in a strict sense: omega is last only in Greek, so the image carries its own alphabet with it into languages whose last letter is something else entirely."},{slug:"good-shepherd",name:"The Good Shepherd",glyph:null,form:"A beardless youth in a short tunic, one sheep across his shoulders, its legs held in his hands; often with a milk-pail, a pipe or a dog, in a landscape of trees and other sheep.",origin:"The commonest single figure in the third-century Roman catacombs, and not a Christian invention. The ram-bearer, kriophoros, is an old Greek type — Hermes carries a ram this way, and the archaic calf-bearer of the Athenian acropolis stands behind them both — while the pastoral landscape belongs to a whole Roman repertoire of bucolic ease used on pagan tombs for the peace of the dead.",meaning:"John 10 and the parable of the lost sheep at Luke 15, read together: the one who searches, and who carries the found animal home himself. In funerary use it is the soul being carried.",usedBy:"Universal in early Christian art; still a central image in every tradition, though it recedes in Byzantine iconography as the Pantocrator and the Deesis take the wall.",note:"The borrowing was almost certainly deliberate rather than furtive: a pagan buyer and a Christian buyer could purchase the same carved shepherd from the same workshop, and only the company it kept made it Christian. By the fifth century the type has been transformed — in the mausoleum of Galla Placidia at Ravenna, c. 425-450, the shepherd sits enthroned in a gold landscape, haloed, holding a cross-staff: pastoral no longer, and imperial instead."},{slug:"orant",name:"The Orant",glyph:null,form:"A standing figure, frontal, arms lifted and bent at the elbow, palms open and outward. In the catacombs the figure is very often a woman, veiled, alone in her frame.",origin:"The ordinary posture of prayer in the ancient Mediterranean, Christian and pagan alike; Roman personifications such as Pietas appear with the hand raised or extended on imperial coinage, in the same family of gesture. Tertullian, in his treatise on prayer, tells Christians to spread the hands but to raise them modestly.",meaning:"Prayer itself, and in tomb painting the soul of the departed at prayer — which is why the orans over a man's grave may be a woman: she is his soul, not his portrait. The gesture also carries the Church interceding, and the cross made by the body.",usedBy:"Retained everywhere as the celebrant's posture at the collects; in the Coptic, Ethiopian and Syriac rites the faithful commonly pray so as well. In Byzantine iconography it becomes the Virgin Orans, the Platytera, filling apses across the Orthodox world.",note:"The recovery of the orans posture by Western congregations in the twentieth century was presented as a return to antiquity, and it is one, but the ancient evidence is for a universal ancient posture rather than for a specifically Christian claim on it. The gesture was never lost in the East."},{slug:"anchor",name:"The Anchor",glyph:null,form:"A ship's anchor, stock and flukes, its shank frequently crossed by a bar so that the upper part reads as a cross; often flanked by two fish, or with a fish twined about the shank.",origin:"Extremely common in the Roman catacombs and on third-century epitaphs, and named by Clement of Alexandria among the permitted seal devices. Its scriptural anchor is Hebrews 6:19, where hope is an anchor of the soul, sure and steadfast.",meaning:"Hope, and steadfastness in death; a ship held against a current that would otherwise carry it off.",usedBy:"Early Christians throughout the Mediterranean; later a saint's attribute, chiefly of Clement of Rome, and a common gravestone device in maritime communities of every denomination.",note:"That the anchor was a cross in disguise, safe to carve while the open cross was dangerous, is a very old and very attractive reading — but it is inference. What can be said is that the anchor becomes markedly rarer in Christian use in the fourth century, roughly as the cross becomes freely displayable, which is at least consistent with the theory. Unicode's ⚓ (U+2693 ANCHOR) belongs to a maritime and typographic lineage and has no descent from the catacomb sign."},{slug:"ship",name:"The Ship",glyph:null,form:"A vessel under sail or oar, the mast and yard forming a cross; sometimes with a figure at the helm, sometimes riding a storm, sometimes with the anchor down.",origin:"Named by Clement of Alexandria among lawful seal devices, and found on lamps, gems and epitaphs. Behind it stand Noah's ark, the stilling of the storm on the lake, and Paul's shipwreck.",meaning:"The Church in passage: a company that cannot get out and swim, carried by something it does not steer. The mast, read as a cross, makes the instrument of the death the thing that catches the wind.",usedBy:"Early Christian funerary art; retained as an emblem by the modern ecumenical movement, whose device is a ship with a cross for a mast on a sea.",note:"The Apostolic Constitutions direct that the church building be long, like a ship, with the bishop at the eastern end — the metaphor turned into a floor plan. The architectural term nave is usually derived from navis, ship, though how far medieval builders had the metaphor consciously in mind is another question."},{slug:"peacock",name:"The Peacock",glyph:null,form:"The bird in profile or displayed, tail fanned and full of eyes; commonly a pair flanking a vase, a vine or a chalice, drinking.",origin:"Roman before it was Christian. The peacock was Juno's bird and the vehicle of the empress's apotheosis on consecratio coinage, carrying the soul upward. It passes into Christian sarcophagi, mosaics and chancel screens in the fourth century and is everywhere in Ravenna.",meaning:"Immortality and the resurrection of the body, on the strength of a belief that the peacock's flesh does not putrefy; the eyes of the tail were later glossed as the all-seeing Church or the vision of God.",usedBy:"Early Christian and Byzantine art throughout the Mediterranean; strongly present in Armenian, Georgian, Coptic and Syriac ornament, and on the chancel screens of Italy.",note:"Augustine reports having tested the claim himself (City of God 21.4): a cooked peacock's breast, kept and inspected after thirty days and again after a year, had dried and shrunk but had not begun to stink — which he offers as an argument from nature that God can preserve a body from corruption. It is one of the very few places where a Father of the Church describes performing an experiment, and the reader should note that he performed it in order to defend a doctrine, not to test the bird."},{slug:"phoenix",name:"The Phoenix",glyph:null,form:"A bird, often haloed and rayed like the sun, standing on or beside a palm tree, sometimes on a pyre of spices.",origin:"A pagan wonder before it was a Christian argument — Herodotus, Pliny and Tacitus all report it. Clement of Rome, writing to Corinth about the year 96, retells the story at length (1 Clement 25) as a sign in nature that God raises the dead. It appears in Christian art from the fourth century, perhaps most memorably in the apse of Santi Cosma e Damiano at Rome, of 526-530, perched in one of the flanking palms.",meaning:"Resurrection, argued from the natural world; and the singularity of the risen life, since there is only ever one phoenix.",usedBy:"Latin and Greek Christian art of late antiquity; later heraldry and emblem books across the confessions.",note:"Clement offers the bird as evidence, which is to say that a first-century Christian pastor was prepared to argue for the resurrection from a creature that does not exist. The poem De ave phoenice, traditionally ascribed to Lactantius, gives the fullest Christian treatment; the ascription is probable but not certain, and the poem itself is not overtly Christian."},{slug:"pelican",name:"The Pelican in her Piety",glyph:null,form:"A bird on a nest, wings raised, bending her long bill against her own breast; below her, three chicks with open mouths. English heraldry blazons the whole group as a pelican in her piety, and the bird alone, wounding herself, as vulning herself.",origin:"A bestiary legend, not a scriptural one. The Physiologus, a Greek compilation of Christianised animal lore probably of Alexandrian origin, tells that the young strike their parents, that the mother kills them, and that after three days she pierces her side and revives them with her own blood. The Latin bestiaries carry it across medieval Europe.",meaning:"Christ giving his blood for the dead, and specifically the eucharist. The three days in the older version of the tale make the reading explicit.",usedBy:"Western medieval and modern Catholic and Anglican art — altar frontals, lecterns, tabernacle doors, and the arms of institutions such as Corpus Christi College, Oxford. Rare in the Christian East.",note:"Flag the legend as legend: pelicans do not do this. They press the bill to the breast to disgorge fish for their young, and in some species the pouch reddens in the breeding season — the two together are the usual explanation offered for how the story began. The eucharistic hymn Adoro te devote, ascribed to Thomas Aquinas, addresses Christ directly as the loving pelican, so the image is doctrinally load-bearing in the Latin West even though its natural history is false."},{slug:"vine",name:"The Vine",glyph:null,form:"A running scroll of vine, tendrils and bunched grapes, frequently inhabited by small figures harvesting and treading; or a single stock with branches spreading from it.",origin:"Both scriptural and Dionysiac. Israel is a vine in Isaiah and the Psalms, and John 15 makes Christ the vine and the disciples the branches; simultaneously, the vintage was one of the standard decorative repertoires of Roman art. In the mausoleum of Santa Costanza at Rome, of about 350, the ambulatory vault is filled with vintaging putti in a manner indistinguishable from pagan work, and read eucharistically by everyone who prays there.",meaning:"Incorporation — the branch has no life apart from the stock — and the eucharistic wine; also the harvest and the winepress of judgement.",usedBy:"Universal. The inhabited vine scroll runs continuously through Coptic, Syriac, Armenian, Georgian and Byzantine ornament and into Umayyad and later Islamic decoration, where the same workshops often carved it.",note:"The Vatican necropolis under St Peter's preserves in Mausoleum M a small vaulted tomb in which vine tendrils surround a charioteer figure with a rayed nimbus, usually read as Christ in the guise of the Sun. Whether such works were understood as translation, as camouflage, or simply as the only visual language available is the standing question of early Christian art, and the vine is its clearest single case."},{slug:"lamb",name:"The Lamb",glyph:null,form:"A standing lamb, often haloed, sometimes with a cross-staff and banner, sometimes on a hill from which four rivers flow, sometimes bleeding into a chalice; or, in Revelation's terms, standing as though slain, with seven horns and seven eyes.",origin:"Exodus 12 and Isaiah 53 behind it; John the Baptist's words at John 1:29 in front of it. Note that the Greek differs: John's gospel uses ἀμνός, Revelation consistently ἀρνίον, a diminutive — the little lamb who opens the scroll.",meaning:"Sacrifice, Passover, and the substitution; in Revelation, also the enthroned victor whom the whole court worships.",usedBy:"Central and continuous in the Latin West — the Agnus Dei of the fraction, the Ghent Altarpiece, the papal pallium woven from the wool of lambs blessed at Rome. Much rarer as an image of Christ in the Christian East.",note:"The divergence is legislated. Canon 82 of the Quinisext Council, held in Trullo at Constantinople in 692, orders that Christ be depicted in his human form and no longer as a lamb, on the grounds that grace and truth are to be preferred to the shadow and the type, and that the depth of God's self-humbling is better seen in the man. Rome did not receive the Quinisext canons — Pope Sergius I refused to subscribe to them — and the Lamb accordingly remained at the centre of Western art and liturgy while Eastern art turned decisively to the face. A neighbouring canon of the same council, 73, forbids setting the cross into pavements, so that it not be trodden underfoot."},{slug:"dove",name:"The Dove",glyph:null,form:"A bird descending, wings spread, usually head downward toward a figure or a font; sometimes with an olive sprig in the beak, sometimes issuing rays, sometimes one of seven.",origin:"The baptism narratives, where the Spirit descends in bodily form as a dove; and Genesis 8, where the dove returns to the ark with an olive leaf. Clement of Alexandria lists the dove among lawful seal devices, so the sign is in Christian use by the late second century.",meaning:"The Holy Spirit; peace and the end of the flood; the soul; and, in groups of seven, the gifts of the Spirit.",usedBy:"Every tradition, though with different reserve: Orthodox iconography admits only two forms for the Spirit — the dove, proper to the Theophany, and the tongues of fire at Pentecost — and shows the dove outside the baptism only rarely, whereas Western art multiplies it freely.",note:"The seven gifts are seven only in Greek and Latin. The Hebrew of Isaiah 11:2 lists six qualities and repeats the fear of the Lord; the Septuagint rendered one occurrence as εὐσέβεια, godliness, and the Vulgate as pietas, giving the seven that the doves depict. The dove that descends at the baptism of Clovis bearing the holy oil belongs to a different order of claim: it is first told by Hincmar of Reims in the later ninth century, nearly four hundred years after the event, and is a legend of the French monarchy before it is anything else."},{slug:"nomina-sacra",name:"The Nomina Sacra",glyph:"Ι̅Σ̅ Χ̅Σ̅",form:"Sacred words written in contraction — usually first and last letters only — under a horizontal stroke: ΘΣ for God, ΚΣ for Lord, ΙΣ for Jesus, ΧΣ for Christ, ΠΝΑ for Spirit. The overline is the mark that makes the contraction sacred rather than merely short.",origin:"Present in effectively every Christian Greek manuscript from the earliest surviving fragments onward, and therefore older than the manuscripts themselves. Ludwig Traube gave the system its name and its first study in 1907. The core four are God, Lord, Jesus and Christ; the list grows to some fifteen, taking in Spirit, cross, Father, Son, Saviour, Mother, heaven, man, Israel, David and Jerusalem.",meaning:"Reverence made visible in the act of copying. It is not abbreviation for economy — the words shortened are not the frequent ones — but a way of setting certain names apart on the page, plausibly modelled on Jewish scribal treatment of the divine Name.",usedBy:"Greek scribes first, then Latin, Coptic, Armenian, Georgian, Gothic, Ethiopic and Slavonic. The system passes intact into icon painting, where ΙϹ ΧϹ and ΜΡ ΘΥ over the figures are a caption and a confession at once.",note:"That the same convention appears across the whole early Christian book trade, in different languages and provinces, is one of the strongest arguments that Christian scribal practice was standardised remarkably early and remarkably widely — a point pressed by Larry Hurtado. An older and stranger relative of the system: the Epistle of Barnabas (9) reads the three hundred and eighteen servants of Abraham as the letters ΤΙΗ — tau for three hundred, the cross; iota and eta for eighteen, the first letters of Jesus — and takes the number as a prophecy."},{slug:"ihs",name:"IHS and IHC",glyph:"IHS",form:"Three letters, often within a blazing sun, often surmounted by a cross rising from the crossbar of the H, often over three nails.",origin:"The first three letters of ΙΗΣΟΥΣ in Greek capitals — iota, eta, sigma. Written with the lunate sigma, ΙΗϹ, they were read by Latin eyes as IHC, and with the ordinary sigma as IHS. The H is a Greek eta and was never an aitch.",meaning:"The name of Jesus, nothing more; the devotion built on it is a devotion to the Holy Name.",usedBy:"The Latin West overwhelmingly — on vestments, hosts, tabernacles, gravestones; and as the emblem of the Society of Jesus, where the sun, the cross and the three nails were added.",note:"The Latin expansions are backronyms devised long after the fact: Iesus Hominum Salvator, In Hoc Signo, Iesum Habemus Socium. They are readings of an abbreviation whose real content is a transliteration. Bernardino of Siena preached across fifteenth-century Italy holding up a tablet with the monogram in a blazing sun, was accused of promoting a novel cult, was brought before the papal court in Rome in 1427 and cleared — the sign survived the process and the Jesuits inherited it."},{slug:"inri",name:"INRI and the Titulus",glyph:"INRI",form:"A small board or scroll fixed above the cross, lettered. In the Latin West, INRI. In the Greek and Slavonic East, most often Ὁ Βασιλεὺς τῆς Δόξης or ЦРЬ СЛВЫ, the King of Glory.",origin:"The charge-board of a Roman execution, naming the crime. All four gospels report one and no two report the same words. Mark 15:26 gives only 'The King of the Jews'; Matthew 27:37, 'This is Jesus, the King of the Jews'; Luke 23:38, 'This is the King of the Jews'; John 19:19, 'Jesus the Nazorean, the King of the Jews'. INRI comes from the Vulgate of John alone — Iesus Nazarenus Rex Iudaeorum.",meaning:"Pilate's sentence, and the gospels' irony: the accusation is the truth, posted by a man who did not believe it. John adds that it was written in Hebrew, Latin and Greek, and that the chief priests asked for the wording to be changed and were refused.",usedBy:"INRI throughout the Latin West and in Western-influenced art everywhere. The Greek and Slavonic East generally replaces the charge with the acclamation from Psalm 24.",note:"The trilingual notice belongs to John. The clause that gives it in Luke 23:38 is absent from the oldest witnesses — P75, Sinaiticus, Vaticanus — and is a scribal harmonisation to John; modern critical editions omit it. A wooden fragment venerated as the titulus at Santa Croce in Gerusalemme, Rome, was radiocarbon dated in a study published in 2002 to somewhere between the tenth and twelfth centuries, which places the object in the medieval period whatever it commemorates."},{slug:"ic-xc-nika",name:"IC XC NIKA",glyph:"ΙϹ ΧϹ ΝΙΚΑ",form:"Four groups of letters set in the four quarters of a square cross: ΙϹ and ΧϹ above, ΝΙ and ΚΑ below. The sigmas are lunate, as in manuscript and icon practice.",origin:"Byzantine. It is above all the device of the prosphora seal — the carved wooden stamp pressed into the loaf before baking, so that the bread arrives at the altar already inscribed and the priest cuts the Lamb out of the square it marks.",meaning:"Jesus Christ conquers. The nomina sacra supply the name; the verb supplies the claim; and on the bread the claim is about to be eaten.",usedBy:"Eastern Orthodox and Byzantine-rite Catholic churches at every liturgy; also on crosses, walls, icons and modern devotional objects.",note:"The Greek is usually read as an indicative, conquers, rather than an imperative, conquer — the two are distinguished in writing by accent and by the iota subscript, neither of which the carved capitals show, and the phrase carries an audible echo of the τούτῳ νίκα that Eusebius reports Constantine seeing. What began as a battle motto ends stamped on bread, which is a fair summary of what the Byzantine liturgy did with imperial language."},{slug:"tetramorph",name:"The Tetramorph",glyph:null,form:"Four living creatures — a man, a lion, an ox and an eagle — winged, usually holding books, set at the four corners around Christ in glory, or fused into a single four-faced being at the wheels of Ezekiel's chariot.",origin:"Ezekiel 1 and Revelation 4 give the four faces; the Fathers assign them to the four gospels. The assignments do not agree. Irenaeus (Against Heresies 3.11.8) gives the lion to John, the ox to Luke, the man to Matthew and the eagle to Mark. Augustine (On the Harmony of the Evangelists 1.6) gives the lion to Matthew, the man to Mark, the ox to Luke and the eagle to John. Jerome, in the preface to his commentary on Matthew, gives the man to Matthew, the lion to Mark, the ox to Luke and the eagle to John — and it is Jerome's arrangement, not Irenaeus's, that won.",meaning:"The fourfold gospel as a single vehicle; and, read allegorically, four aspects of Christ — the incarnation, the royal power, the sacrifice and the ascension.",usedBy:"Universal: Latin apses and Insular gospel books, Byzantine domes, Armenian and Ethiopian manuscript frontispieces, Syriac lectionaries.",note:"The disagreement is not a small one and it is not hidden. All three writers reason from the text rather than from received tradition — chiefly from how each gospel opens and from the character each opening sets — and they reach three different results, which is itself the point: the assignment was argued into place, not inherited. Where a medieval work assigns the creatures against Jerome, it is usually a sign of a deliberately archaic source, which makes the tetramorph one of the few symbols whose variant readings can help to date and place a manuscript."},{slug:"deesis",name:"The Deesis",glyph:null,form:"Three figures. Christ enthroned or in bust at the centre; the Mother of God at his right hand and John the Forerunner at his left, both turned inward, heads inclined, hands raised toward him in petition.",origin:"A Byzantine composition, developed from the fifth or sixth century onward and standard by the middle Byzantine period. Its supreme surviving example is the mosaic in the south gallery of Hagia Sophia, usually dated after the recovery of Constantinople in 1261, of which only the upper portion survives.",meaning:"Intercession. δέησις means entreaty. The two greatest of the created — the one who bore him and the one who baptised him — are shown doing the one thing the created can do, and the viewer is placed behind them in the same posture.",usedBy:"Eastern Orthodox and Byzantine-rite churches, where the Deesis is the central and governing tier of the iconostasis, expanded with archangels, apostles and hierarchs all turned toward the centre. It passes into Western art through Italian panel painting.",note:"Art historians, Christopher Walter among them, have observed that Deesis as the name of this specific composition is a modern art-historical label; Byzantine writers used the word more loosely for prayer and petition of many kinds. The convention that the Virgin stands at Christ's right and the Forerunner at his left is nearly invariable, and a reversal in a given work is worth pausing over rather than assuming to be error."},{slug:"pantocrator",name:"The Pantocrator",glyph:null,form:"Christ in bust or enthroned, frontal, gazing out. The right hand blesses; the left holds a book, closed and jewelled or open at a text. A cruciform nimbus surrounds the head, and ΙϹ ΧϹ stands in the upper field.",origin:"Παντοκράτωρ is the Septuagint's word for the Hebrew titles of hosts and of the Almighty, and stands in the Greek creed where the Latin has omnipotentem. The iconographic type is fixed by the sixth century — the encaustic panel at the monastery of St Catherine on Sinai is the great survivor — and takes the dome in the middle Byzantine period: Daphni, Cefalù in 1148, Monreale.",meaning:"Ruler of all: sovereignty rather than fabrication. The type places the judge overhead and looking down, and the book in the hand is the standard by which the looking is done.",usedBy:"Eastern Orthodox and Oriental Orthodox churches, Byzantine-rite Catholics, and, through the Norman mosaics of Sicily, the Latin West.",note:"Two much-repeated readings should be labelled as readings. That the Sinai panel's asymmetric halves depict the two natures is a modern interpretation of a face that may simply be painted by a hand working freely. That the blessing hand's fingers deliberately form the letters ΙϹ ΧϹ is a later devotional gloss on a gesture whose origins are in Roman rhetorical convention. Both are held sincerely by many who pray before these images, and neither is documented in the period that produced them."},{slug:"cruciform-nimbus",name:"The Cruciform Nimbus",glyph:"Ὁ ὬΝ",form:"A halo with the arms of a cross inscribed within it, three arms visible behind the head. In Greek practice each visible arm carries one letter of Ὁ ὬΝ — Ο, Ω, Ν — read around the head.",origin:"The plain nimbus is pre-Christian, a mark of radiance used for Sol, for personified powers and for emperors. Christian art restricts the cross-in-halo to the persons of the Trinity, and the Byzantine convention adds the letters, which render the Septuagint of Exodus 3:14: ὁ ὤν, the One Who Is, the name given at the bush.",meaning:"Divinity, distinguished from sanctity: saints have haloes, only God has this one. The letters make the identification of the figure with the God of Sinai explicit and unarguable.",usedBy:"Eastern Orthodox and Oriental Orthodox iconography without exception; the Latin West uses the cruciform nimbus but generally without the letters.",note:"Neighbouring conventions repay attention. A square nimbus marks a living person: Pope Paschal I appears with one in the apse mosaic of Santa Prassede in Rome, made in his own lifetime between 817 and 824, which is why the square is effectively a date stamp. A triangular nimbus for the Father is a later Western device. And the almond-shaped mandorla, which surrounds the whole body rather than the head, appears at the Transfiguration, the Ascension and the Anastasis — as in the parekklesion of the Chora church at Constantinople, of about 1315-21. In much Palaiologan painting the mandorla is built of graded rings that darken toward the centre, so that uncreated light is shown as darkness; the convention is clearest in Transfiguration icons of the period."},{slug:"old-believer-cross",name:"The Eight-Pointed Cross",glyph:"☦︎",form:"Three bars. A short upper bar for the title-board; the long arms; and a lower slanting bar for the footrest, its right end (the viewer's left) raised. Around it, in Golgotha crosses, a dense apparatus of letters: К and Т for the lance and the reed, ГГ for the hill of Golgotha, ГА for Adam's skull, МЛРБ for the place of the skull become paradise, and НИКА.",origin:"A Byzantine and Slavic development in which the suppedaneum of early crucifixion images becomes a fixed and slanted bar. It becomes a confessional marker in Russia after the liturgical reforms pressed by Patriarch Nikon from 1653 and the anathemas of the councils of 1666-67, when those who kept the old rite kept the old cross.",meaning:"The slant is read devotionally as the balance of the two thieves — raised toward the one who repented, lowered toward the one who did not — following the hymnography of the Good Thief. Other explanations, including the flattening of a perspectival footrest in early images, have been offered and cannot be excluded.",usedBy:"The Russian Orthodox Church and other Slavic Orthodox churches generally, and above all the Old Believers, who call themselves starovery or staroobryadtsy and for whom the eight-pointed form is not a preference but a boundary; the four-pointed 'Latin' cross is rejected by many concords.",note:"Some priestless Old Believer concords replace the ІНЦІ of Pilate's board with ЦРЬ СЛВЫ, the King of Glory, holding that the executioner's charge has no place on the cross that is venerated. The old name raskolniki, schismatics, is a state-church exonym and should not be used of them. The anathemas laid on the old rites in 1666-67 were formally lifted by the Moscow Patriarchate at its council of 1971, and by the Russian Church Outside Russia in 1974. Unicode's name for the glyph, U+2626 ORTHODOX CROSS, is a modern label of convenience rather than any tradition's own term."},{slug:"jerusalem-cross",name:"The Jerusalem Cross",glyph:"☩",form:"A large cross potent — its arms ending in crossbars — with a smaller plain cross set in each of the four quarters. Five crosses in all.",origin:"The arms of the Latin Kingdom of Jerusalem. The attribution to Godfrey of Bouillon is legendary; the arms are first securely attested rather later. The Franciscan Custody of the Holy Land carries it still, and it is the badge that pilgrims brought home.",meaning:"Read variously as the five wounds; as Christ with the four evangelists; and as the gospel going out from Jerusalem to the four quarters of the earth. All three readings are later than the device, and none is its documented origin.",usedBy:"The Franciscan Custody, the Latin Patriarchate of Jerusalem, the Anglican and Episcopal churches as a decorative device, and pilgrims of every tradition as a tattoo, a badge or a medal.",note:"The blazon is a famous scandal of heraldry: gold crosses on a silver field, metal upon metal, breaking the rule of tincture that governs almost everything else in the discipline. The usual explanation is that the arms of the holy city were held to stand outside ordinary law — an explanation which may be a rationalisation of an anomaly whose real cause is unknown."},{slug:"tau-francis",name:"The Tau",glyph:"Τ",form:"A cross with no upright above the transom: the Greek letter tau, or the Hebrew tav of the old script, which in the archaic alphabet was itself written as a cross.",origin:"Ezekiel 9:4, where a mark is set on the foreheads of those who grieve over the city's sins; the Vulgate renders the Hebrew word as thau, so that Latin readers found the letter itself in the text. Innocent III opened the Fourth Lateran Council in 1215 with a sermon calling for that mark to be borne, and Francis of Assisi took the letter as his own sign and his signature.",meaning:"The mark of those who are spared; penance and the cross reduced to a single stroke of the pen.",usedBy:"Franciscans of every branch, and the wider Franciscan family, as habit-cord pendant and emblem. Also the Antonines, hospitallers of St Anthony, who wore the tau on their habits and cared for sufferers of the ergotism called St Anthony's fire.",note:"The small parchment known as the Chartula, kept at the Sacro Convento in Assisi, carries on one side the Praises of the Most High God and on the other a blessing for Brother Leo, with a tau drawn through a head by Francis's own hand — one of the very few objects surviving that he is held to have written and drawn himself. The tau is also, incidentally, the letter Barnabas read as three hundred in Abraham's three hundred and eighteen servants, which makes it the oldest attested Christian reading of a letter as a cross."},{slug:"celtic-ring-cross",name:"The Ring Cross",glyph:"🕈",form:"A cross whose arms are joined by a circle passing through them near the crossing, standing on a shaped base and, on the great monuments, carved over its whole surface with interlace, spirals, bosses and scriptural scenes.",origin:"The high crosses of Ireland, western Britain and Iona, from roughly the eighth century to the twelfth: Ahenny, Moone, the Cross of the Scriptures at Clonmacnoise, Muiredach's Cross at Monasterboice, whose inscription asks a prayer for the Muiredach who had it made.",meaning:"A preaching monument, a boundary marker, a place of assembly and burial, and a scripture cycle in stone for a population that could not read.",usedBy:"The Irish, Scottish and Welsh churches of the early medieval period; today by Catholic, Anglican, Presbyterian and secular Ireland, Scotland and their diasporas alike.",note:"The ring's origin is genuinely unresolved and every account of it should be hedged. The proposals that carry most scholarly weight are structural — the ring supports the projecting arms of a stone cross — and iconographic, the ring deriving from the laurel wreath that surrounds the Chi-Rho on late Roman victory monuments. The popular explanation, that it is a pre-Christian sun disc over which the cross was laid, has little early evidence behind it, and the story that St Patrick made the combination himself appears in none of the early Lives. Two further honesties: the term Celtic cross is a nineteenth-century coinage of the Celtic Revival — Unicode has adopted it too, naming U+1F548 CELTIC CROSS — and the form has since been taken up as an emblem by white-nationalist groups, which now shadows its use outside its home."},{slug:"khachkar",name:"The Khachkar",glyph:null,form:"An upright slab of tuff or basalt, worked in low relief: a cross rising from a rosette or disc, its arms terminating in buds or trefoils, surrounded by a lattice of vegetal and geometric interlace, the whole often under a carved arch. The crucified body is almost never shown.",origin:"Armenian, from the ninth century. The earliest bearing a date is the khachkar raised by Queen Katranide at Garni in 879. The art reaches its height in the thirteenth century — the Amenaprkich khachkar at Goshavank, carved by the master Poghos in 1291, is the acknowledged summit.",meaning:"Not primarily a gravestone. A khachkar is votive: raised for the salvation of a living person, in thanks for a victory, at the founding of a bridge, a spring or a church. The cross is shown blossoming, growing from the earth as a tree.",usedBy:"The Armenian Apostolic Church, and the Armenian Catholic and Evangelical communities; the form is unmistakably and exclusively Armenian, and functions as a national as well as a religious mark.",note:"UNESCO inscribed the art of the khachkar on its list of intangible cultural heritage in 2010. The great field of khachkars at Julfa in Nakhchivan, which once held thousands, was destroyed; the final clearance was filmed from across the Araxes in 2005, and the responsibility is disputed by Azerbaijan. The loss is the largest single destruction of Armenian Christian material culture in modern times."},{slug:"ethiopian-cross",name:"The Ethiopian Cross",glyph:null,form:"Openwork lattice: a cross whose form is generated by an unbroken interlaced band, arms folding out into further crosses, the whole often set on a small square base. Hand crosses of brass or silver, tall processional crosses, neck crosses in dozens of regional patterns — Lalibela, Gondar, Axum, Shoa, Lasta.",origin:"Ethiopian and Eritrean, developed from Axumite and Coptic forms and elaborated across a millennium of metalworking. The rock-hewn church of Bete Giyorgis at Lalibela is itself cut in the shape of a cross, seen whole only from above.",meaning:"The unbroken interlace is read as eternity, having no beginning and no end, and the openwork as the net that draws in the world. The square block at the base of many hand crosses is explained as the tomb of Adam, or as the tabot — the ark that stands in every Ethiopian church — and the two explanations are both current.",usedBy:"The Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church and the Eritrean Orthodox Tewahedo Church, whose priests carry hand crosses to be kissed by the faithful; also the Ethiopian Catholic Church, and, as a cultural and national form, among Ethiopian Christians of other communities including the P'ent'ay churches.",note:"Tewahedo means made one, and names the church's own Christology, which is miaphysite: one incarnate nature of the Word, from two, without confusion. Monophysite is a polemical exonym and is refused by these churches. Their feast of the cross, Mäsqäl, kept on 17 Mäskäräm — 27 September, 28 in the year before a leap year — with the great bonfire called the demera, was inscribed by UNESCO on the intangible heritage list in 2013."},{slug:"saint-thomas-cross",name:"The Saint Thomas Cross",glyph:null,form:"A plain equal-armed cross of granite in low relief, the arms flaring and ending in buds; it rises from a lotus, stands within an arch, and is often crowned by a descending dove. Pahlavi inscriptions run along the arch. There is no body on it.",origin:"Southern India, most probably between the seventh and ninth centuries. Examples survive at St Thomas Mount in Chennai and in Kerala at Kottayam — where two stand in the Valiya Palli — and at Muttuchira, Kadamattom, Kothanalloor and Alangad.",meaning:"The cross flowering out of the lotus: the resurrection stated in the visual language of the country it is standing in, without apology and without a corpus.",usedBy:"The Saint Thomas Christians of Kerala — the Mar Thoma Nasranis — whose descendants are today divided among the Syro-Malabar and Syro-Malankara Catholic churches, the Malankara Orthodox and Jacobite Syrian churches, the Mar Thoma Church, the Chaldean Syrian Church and others.",note:"Two accounts stand side by side. The community's own tradition holds that the Apostle Thomas landed at Muziris in the year 52, founded the seven and a half churches remembered as the Ezharappallikal, and was martyred near Mylapore; historians treat the tradition as ancient and the date as traditional rather than documented, while noting that Roman-period trade between Egypt and the Malabar coast makes the journey entirely feasible. The Pahlavi inscriptions have been read differently by different epigraphers; C. P. T. Winckworth's reading of 1929, giving a plea for mercy on the man who cut the stone, remains the most cited, and the differences between readings are substantial enough to matter."},{slug:"church-of-east-cross",name:"The Cross of the Church of the East",glyph:null,form:"An equal-armed cross with flaring arms, pearled or beaded at the terminals, rising from a lotus, a cloud or a stepped base; sometimes flanked by angels in Chinese dress, sometimes set above a Syriac inscription. Never with a corpus.",origin:"The Church of the East carried its cross along the Silk Road. The Xi'an stele of 781, recording the propagation in China of the Luminous Religion from Daqin, bears such a cross at its head above a lotus and cloud; it was unearthed in the 1620s and stands now in the Beilin Museum. The Yuan-period carved crosses of Quanzhou, in Syriac, Turkic and Chinese, show the same cross carried by angels or set above a lotus.",meaning:"Glory rather than suffering: this tradition's crosses show the victory and not the death, and the lotus beneath is not a compromise but a translation.",usedBy:"The Assyrian Church of the East, the Ancient Church of the East, and the Chaldean Catholic Church; historically a communion stretching from Mesopotamia to Kerala, Central Asia, Mongolia and China.",note:"Nestorian is a polemical exonym imposed from outside and is refused by this church, which has never named itself so. Nestorius, Archbishop of Constantinople, objected to the title Theotokos for the Virgin and preferred Christotokos, and was condemned at Ephesus in 431; the Church of the East honours him among its teachers but did not take its Christology from him, and its own formula speaks of two qnome in one parsopa — the Syriac term whose Greek equivalent is prosopon. The Common Christological Declaration signed in 1994 by John Paul II and Mar Dinkha IV recognised that the two churches confess the same faith in different theological languages."},{slug:"grapevine-cross",name:"The Grapevine Cross",glyph:null,form:"A cross whose horizontal arms droop at the ends, as a living vine bends under its own weight; the wood bound at the crossing.",origin:"Georgian. The tradition holds that the Mother of God appeared to Nino before she set out for Kartli and gave her a cross made of vine cuttings, which Nino bound at the centre with her own hair. The relic venerated as that cross is kept at Sioni Cathedral in Tbilisi; its documented history is medieval and later, it was carried to Russia during the upheavals of the eighteenth century, and it was returned to Tbilisi in the early nineteenth.",meaning:"The vine of John 15 made into the cross itself; and, for Georgians, the conversion of the country as a single object one can look at.",usedBy:"The Georgian Apostolic Autocephalous Orthodox Church, on domes, in hands, around necks, and as a national emblem.",note:"The tradition's account and the historians' account are both worth setting down. Georgian sources name Nino, tell of her preaching at Mtskheta and the healing of Queen Nana, and honour her as Equal-to-the-Apostles. The earliest surviving narrative of the conversion of Iberia, in Rufinus's Ecclesiastical History, tells of a Christian captive woman whose prayers healed a child and then the queen, and who brought the kingdom to the faith — and does not give her a name."},{slug:"coptic-tattoo",name:"The Pilgrim's Tattoo",glyph:null,form:"A small cross, most often on the inside of the right wrist. Pilgrims to Jerusalem take larger and more elaborate designs — the Jerusalem cross, the resurrection, a saint — and traditionally add the year of the pilgrimage.",origin:"Long attested among the Copts of Egypt, where the wrist cross is given in childhood and functions as an inalienable mark of belonging. Pilgrim tattooing at the Holy Sepulchre is described by European travellers from the early modern period onward, with the design pricked from a carved wooden stamp block.",meaning:"Identity that cannot be taken off, mislaid or denied under pressure; and, for the pilgrim, the journey recorded on the body that made it.",usedBy:"Coptic Orthodox Christians above all; also Ethiopian, Armenian, Syriac and Latin pilgrims to Jerusalem, and Bosnian Catholic women, whose sicanje bore its own regional forms.",note:"The practice sits against Leviticus 19:28, and the traditions answer with Revelation's seal on the foreheads of the servants of God and with Paul's declaration that he bears the marks of Jesus on his body — their reasoning, given in their terms. The Razzouk family of Jerusalem, Coptic in origin, tattoo pilgrims still; their own account gives the family some seven centuries in the trade, and among their working stamps is a block dated 1749."},{slug:"liturgical-colours",name:"The Liturgical Colours",glyph:null,form:"White or gold, red, green, violet, black, and rose — worn as chasuble, stole, veil and frontal, and changed with the day.",origin:"A late and Western system. Lotario dei Segni, before he became Innocent III, describes in his treatise on the mystery of the altar a Roman use of four principal colours — white, red, green and black — with violet as a substitute; that is at the end of the twelfth century, more than a millennium into the history of Christian worship. The scheme was made uniform for the Roman Rite only by the missal of Pius V in 1570.",meaning:"White for feasts of the Lord and of the unmartyred; red for the Spirit, the passion and the blood of martyrs; green for the ordinary weeks; violet for Advent and Lent; black for the dead; rose for the two Sundays of relief within the penitential seasons.",usedBy:"The Roman Rite and, by adoption, most Anglican, Lutheran and Methodist churches. The Byzantine rite has no comparable canon: local custom governs, bright for feasts and dark for Lent, with white the colour of Pascha and — in Russian use — of funerals.",note:"The Slavic assignment of blue to the feasts of the Theotokos and of gold as an everyday colour is modern custom and not law; usage differs between and within the Orthodox churches. The medieval English use of Sarum ran red where Rome ran green, and kept the Lenten array of unbleached linen with red or blue emblems, a scheme recovered by Anglicans in the nineteenth century. Coptic, Armenian, Ethiopian and Syriac vesture follows its own logic entirely, and it is a mistake to describe any of them as a variation on the Roman list."},{slug:"baptistery-octagon",name:"The Octagon and the Eighth Day",glyph:null,form:"A building of eight sides, with an eight-sided font sunk at the centre and steps down into it and up out the other side; the walls above rising to a dome.",origin:"The plan is standard for baptisteries by the fifth century — San Giovanni alle Fonti at Milan, the Lateran baptistery rebuilt octagonal under Sixtus III between 432 and 440, and both baptisteries at Ravenna, the Neonian and the one conventionally called the Arian, built for the Gothic Homoian community whom their opponents named after Arius; later, the great octagon of San Giovanni at Florence. The verses ascribed to Ambrose for the Milan font state the reasoning plainly: that it was fitting for the hall of holy baptism to rise on that number, by which salvation returned to the peoples in the light of the risen Christ.",meaning:"The eighth day. The week has seven; the day after the sabbath is both the first and, counted onward, the eighth — the day of the resurrection and of a creation that begins again outside the old count. To descend into an eight-sided pool is to be buried on the seventh day and to come out on the eighth.",usedBy:"Latin and Byzantine baptisteries of late antiquity, and the octagonal font of medieval and modern Western churches; the number recurs in Byzantine and Armenian church plans and in the eight-pointed star of Orthodox and Coptic ornament.",note:"The theology is older than the buildings. The Epistle of Barnabas (15) already contrasts the seventh day with the eighth, on which a new world begins; Justin Martyr, arguing with Trypho, reads both the eight souls saved in the ark and the circumcision on the eighth day as figures of the same thing. The number arrives at the font from a numerical argument about time, and only afterwards becomes an architectural habit — which is why octagonal baptisteries appear across the empire at roughly the same moment, in places that were not copying one another's floor plans."}]};export{e as default};
